        "id": "feedstock-security",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Large gas resources do not guarantee continuous supply",
        "content": "Iran’s gas resources must serve competing demands. EIA describes seasonal pressure from heating and electricity generation, inadequate storage, and investment and technology constraints affecting upstream development. Its assessment explains why a country with substantial reserves can still experience industrial supply shortages. Reserves describe a resource underground; reliable plant deliveries depend on production, processing, transport and demand management. [s1]\n\nEIA also identifies gas capture and processing projects intended partly to supply petrochemical users. Such projects should be read as dated development descriptions: an announced commissioning date does not establish that a facility was completed or delivered its intended feedstock. [s1]",
